-- feed_communities (05-03) — the moment `feed_posts.community_id`, reserved with its own index in
-- Phase 4 and docblocked as Phase 5's inheritance, becomes a real column on both sides: the FOREIGN
-- KEY it never had, and the THIRD INDEX the merged feed turned out to need.
--
-- THE FILE IS TWO HALVES, and the split is deliberate:
--
--   1. GENERATED by `drizzle-kit generate` from `packages/modules/feed/db/schema.ts` — the
--      `feed_posts_tenant_created_all_idx` statement below, byte for byte. `pnpm db:generate` is a
--      no-op against it, which is the property that keeps the TS schema and this folder honest.
--
--   2. HAND-WRITTEN — `feed_posts_community_fk`. It is not generated because it CANNOT be: the
--      referenced table belongs to `@rede-social/module-communities`, and `turbo.json`'s boundary
--      allowlist denies a `module -> module` package dependency (MOD-02, proved by
--      `packages/boundary-fixture`). A drizzle `.references(() => communities.id)` needs that
--      import, so the constraint is declared here instead. Nothing is lost: the DATABASE enforces
--      it either way, `supabase/tests/090-feed.sql` asserts both directions with a positive
--      control, and drizzle never diffs it away — the constraint is absent from the TS schema and
--      therefore absent from the snapshot `drizzle-kit generate` compares against.
--
-- WHY THE CONSTRAINT IS VALIDATING AND NOT `NOT VALID`: adding a foreign key takes a
-- `SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE` lock and scans the child table to validate the existing rows. At this
-- migration every `feed_posts.community_id` in every environment is NULL — the column has never had
-- a writer (05-03 is what adds one) and a NULL child never participates in a referential check — so
-- the validation is trivial and the lock is momentary. Deferring it with `NOT VALID` would buy
-- nothing and would leave a constraint that is not yet a guarantee.
--
-- WHY THERE IS NO `ON DELETE` CLAUSE: the default `NO ACTION` is the decision. A community may never
-- silently take its posts with it — ARCHIVE is the supported disappearance (05-RESEARCH §Pattern 7:
-- the posts stay in the feed, still labelled and still reachable) and COMM-01 offers no deletion at
-- all. A `cascade` here would make a future "delete community" button a silent bulk post delete;
-- `set null` would silently re-home every one of them into the tenant-wide feed. Both are product
-- decisions this migration declines to pre-approve, so the database refuses the delete instead.
--
-- WHY A THIRD INDEX (the measured half, 05-RESEARCH §Pattern 3): D-73 assumed the merged feed was
-- served by the composite `feed_posts_tenant_community_created_idx` Phase 4 built. It is not. With a
-- 500-post fixture and `analyze`, `where tenant_id = $1 and deleted_at is null order by created_at
-- desc, id desc limit 10` plans as `Sort` + `Seq Scan`: `community_id` is the second key column and,
-- with no equality pinning it and no way to drop it from the key, it cannot be skipped. The
-- predicate therefore has to leave the key entirely, which is this index. All THREE indexes on
-- `feed_posts` are now justified and none is redundant:
--   feed_posts_tenant_created_all_idx        -> the merged feed, communities module ON  (D-73)
--   feed_posts_tenant_created_idx (partial)  -> the same feed with the module OFF        (D-74)
--   feed_posts_tenant_community_created_idx  -> one community's own page                 (COMM-03)
-- `090-feed.sql` pins the first two plans on a volume fixture, so dropping either one turns the
-- pgTAP suite red rather than turning the pilot's feed into a sequential scan nobody notices.
CREATE INDEX "feed_posts_tenant_created_all_idx" ON "feed_posts" USING btree ("tenant_id","created_at" DESC NULLS FIRST,"id" DESC NULLS FIRST);--> statement-breakpoint
-- half 2, hand-written: the foreign key from feed_posts.community_id to public.communities(id).
ALTER TABLE "public"."feed_posts"
  ADD CONSTRAINT "feed_posts_community_fk"
  FOREIGN KEY ("community_id") REFERENCES "public"."communities"("id");
